description:
Found 2026-07-28 from LLO while mapping what could move to LLO's schema-bridge.
vigil concurs this is the higher-value finding of that investigation.

## The defect

`scripts/build-manifest.mjs:534` `backendFromGeneratedRow()`:

    if (!row || typeof row !== "object") {
      console.error(`skipping malformed generated_backends row: …`); return null;
    }
    if (typeof row.name !== "string" || row.name === "") {
      console.error(`skipping row with missing name: …`);           return null;
    }
    const handlesPrefix  = typeof row.handlesPrefix  === "string" ? row.handlesPrefix  : "";
    const stripPrefix    = typeof row.stripPrefix    === "string" ? row.stripPrefix    : "";
    const urlBinding     = typeof row.urlBinding     === "string" ? row.urlBinding     : "";
    const dynamicTools   = row.dynamicTools !== false;
    const claims         = Array.isArray(row.claims) ? row.claims.slice() : [];

A malformed row is **skipped, logged to stderr, and the build exits 0** — so
the generated manifest silently omits a backend. Every route that backend
served 404s at runtime, from a build that reported success.

## Why coercion is not merely sloppy here — it is ambiguous

`cluster.lock.toml` contains, in a real row:

    [[generated_backends]]
    input = "llo"
    name = "lsp"
    handlesPrefix = "lsp_"
    stripPrefix = ""          # <-- legitimately empty
    claims = [ "lsp_hover", … ]

`stripPrefix = ""` is a **meaningful value**, so `""` cannot also serve as the
"field absent or wrong type" sentinel. The coercion makes those two states
indistinguishable by construction.

Worse: **unknown keys are silently ignored.** `input` is present in every real
row and read by nothing here. So `handlesPrefixx = "lsp_"` (one typo) is
accepted without complaint and the backend gets `handlesPrefix: ""`, matching
nothing. `claim = [...]` instead of `claims` yields a route claiming zero tools.
`dynamicTool = false` leaves `dynamicTools` true. None of these produce a
diagnostic.

## The mechanism already exists, 250 lines up, in this file

`build-manifest.mjs:277`:

    function fail(msg) {
      console.error(`build-manifest: validation failed — ${msg}`);
      process.exit(2);
    }

and `validate()` uses it for precisely this class:

    if (typeof r.path !== "string") fail(`route.path missing on …`);
    if (seenPaths.has(r.path))      fail(`duplicate route path: …`);
    if (!r.kind || typeof r.kind !== "object") fail(`route.kind missing on …`);

Note `backendFromGeneratedRow` DOES already `fail()` on a name collision
(line ~458). So the file is internally inconsistent: a duplicate name is fatal,
a malformed row is a shrug.

## Immediate fix (small, shippable now)

Replace every `console.error(…); return null` with `fail(…)`, drop the
coercions in favour of explicit type assertions, and **reject unknown keys** so
a typo is an error rather than a silent default:

    const KNOWN = new Set(["input","name","handlesPrefix","stripPrefix",
                           "urlBinding","serviceBinding","dynamicTools",
                           "requiresSession","claims"]);

    function backendFromGeneratedRow(row) {
      if (!row || typeof row !== "object")
        fail(`generated_backends row is not a table: ${JSON.stringify(row)}`);
      for (const k of Object.keys(row))
        if (!KNOWN.has(k))
          fail(`generated_backends row "${row.name ?? "?"}" has unknown key "${k}" ` +
               `— a typo here silently yields a default. Known: ${[...KNOWN].join(", ")}`);
      if (typeof row.name !== "string" || row.name === "")
        fail(`generated_backends row has no name: ${JSON.stringify(row)}`);
      // Absent is fine and means the documented default; WRONG-TYPED is not.
      const str = (k, dflt) => {
        if (row[k] === undefined) return dflt;
        if (typeof row[k] !== "string")
          fail(`generated_backends "${row.name}".${k} must be a string, got ${typeof row[k]}`);
        return row[k];
      };
      …

Keeping absent-means-default is deliberate: it preserves back-compat with
pre-P3 lockfiles, which is why the coercions existed. What changes is that a
PRESENT-but-wrong value, or an unknown key, becomes fatal.

## Structural fix (the real one)

The root cause is that the `generated_backends` row shape is **nowhere
declared**, so the author had no schema to validate against and could only
coerce. Declare it in `manifest/cluster.capnp` and generate its validator with
the zod emitter this repo already consumes from LLO (`capnpc-schema-bridge-zod`,
which hard-errors on unmapped constructs rather than emitting `z.unknown()`).
Then the fail-open path is not fixed, it is **unrepresentable** — the check
ceases to exist rather than passing, per cloister-86ce1f.

That also removes the hand-maintained `KNOWN` set above, which is itself a
second statement of the shape that can drift from the first.

## Acceptance criteria

`task manifest` **exits non-zero** for: a row with an unknown key, a row whose
`handlesPrefix` is a non-string, and a row missing `name`. Proven by feeding
each malformed row in and checking the exit code — not by reading the code.
And the existing `cluster.lock.toml` still builds byte-identically, so the
tightening is provably not a behaviour change for valid input.
